Purpose: Extension (3rdparty) policies no longer make the browser look enterprise-managed. Corvid uses a policy file only to configure preinstalled extensions (uBO autoUpdate off); without this the menu, the extensions page and settings show "Managed by your organization".
Area: debrand
Files: components/policy/core/browser/browser_policy_connector.cc (BrowserPolicyConnector::ProviderHasPolicies)
Rebase risk: low
Origin: original (written for Corvid; no third-party patch text reused)
Status: BUILT and checked on 155.0.8059.39 (2026-10-18): with /etc/corvid/policies/managed/corvid-preinstalled.json present the extensions page shows no managed banner and the app menu has no managed entry. Real browser-level policies still mark the browser managed.

diff --git a/components/policy/core/browser/browser_policy_connector.cc b/components/policy/core/browser/browser_policy_connector.cc
index 6f234c0d5d24..0a061d0eba99 100644
--- a/components/policy/core/browser/browser_policy_connector.cc
+++ b/components/policy/core/browser/browser_policy_connector.cc
@@ -94,6 +94,10 @@ bool BrowserPolicyConnector::ProviderHasPolicies(
     return false;
   }
   for (const auto& pair : provider->policies()) {
+    // Browser patch: only browser-level (Chrome domain) policies make the browser "managed". Extension policies
+    // (3rdparty), used here to configure preinstalled extensions, do not.
+    if (pair.first.domain != POLICY_DOMAIN_CHROME)
+      continue;
     if (!pair.second.empty())
       return true;
   }
